Description: M52XQ: optionally keep NetworkManager awake over a system suspend
 On the phone (s2idle, Wi-Fi kept associated with Wake-on-WLAN, modem handled by ModemManager) every suspend put
 NetworkManager into "asleep" (state DISABLED) and back. Applications (Telegram) took that for a network loss and
 reconnected all sockets on every sleep and every wake-up; the answers of their servers woke the phone again every
 ~15 s. With the file /etc/NetworkManager/m52xq-ignore-suspend NetworkManager ignores the logind sleep/resume signal
 (the power monitor still drops its delay inhibitor at once, so the suspend is not delayed). Remove the file to get
 the usual behaviour back.
Author: Asdlerto Korry <korryasdlerto@gmail.com>
--- a/src/core/nm-manager.c
+++ b/src/core/nm-manager.c
@@ -7567,6 +7567,12 @@
     NMManager *self = user_data;
 
     _LOGT(LOGD_SUSPEND, "sleep: received %s signal", is_about_to_suspend ? "sleeping" : "resuming");
+    if (g_file_test("/etc/NetworkManager/m52xq-ignore-suspend", G_FILE_TEST_EXISTS)) {
+        _LOGI(LOGD_SUSPEND,
+              "sleep: m52xq: ignoring the %s signal (/etc/NetworkManager/m52xq-ignore-suspend)",
+              is_about_to_suspend ? "sleeping" : "resuming");
+        return;
+    }
     _internal_sleep(self, is_about_to_suspend);
 }
 
